A change on the way we write an equilibrium reaction will have an impact on the expression of the equilibrium constant, that is the formula representing this constant.

Let us use as an example the \emph{Sabatier reaction} that produces methane and water by reacting carbon dioxide with hydrogen at high temperatures and pressures over a metal catalyst
\\\ce{CO2_{(g)} + 4H2_{(g)} <=> CH4_{(g)} + 2H2O_{(g)}    }\hfill $K_{c}(A\ce{<=>}B)=\frac{   \big[ \ce{CH4} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{H2O} \big]^2    } {  \big[ \ce{CO2} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{H2} \big]^4    }$\\
The reverse of the Sabatier process is called the \emph{steam methane reforming} (or thermal decomposition/pyrolysis of methane), which breaks down methane and water into carbon dioxide and hydrogen. Its equilibrium constant is given by
\\\ce{CH4_{(g)} + 2H2O_{(g)}  <=>CO2_{(g)} + 4H2_{(g)}    }\hfill $K_{c}(B\ce{<=>}A)=\frac{  \big[ \ce{CO2} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{H2} \big]^4      } {\big[ \ce{CH4} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{H2O} \big]^2      }$\\
We can see that
\begin{equation*}
 \boxed{ 
  K_{c}(A\ce{<=>}B)=\frac{  1     } { K_{c}(B\ce{<=>}A)   }
  }
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we can conclude that reverting an equilibrium inverts its equilibrium constant expression.

Simmilarly, if we multiply an equilibrium by a number, we also impact the expression of the equilibrium constant. For example:
\\\ce{2CO2_{(g)} + 8H2_{(g)} <=> 2CH4_{(g)} + 4H2O_{(g)}     }\hfill $K_{c}(2A\ce{<=>}2B)=\frac{   \big[ \ce{CH4} \big]^2 \cdot \big[ \ce{H2O} \big]^4    } {  \big[ \ce{CO2} \big]^2 \cdot \big[ \ce{H2} \big]^8    }$\\
By comparing $K_{c,A}$ and $K_{c,C}$ we can see that if we time the chemical equilibrium by a number we introduce a power on the equilibrium expression, and we have
\begin{equation*}
 \boxed{ 
  K_{c}(2A\ce{<=>}2B)=(K_{c}(A\ce{<=>}B))^2
  }
\end{equation*}
Finally, let us address the case in which we add two equilibriums. On one hand, we have the Sabatier reacion producing steam (reaction (1) below), but at a the right temperature this steam can liquify producing water (reaction (2) below). The result of adding both reactions would be the Sabatier reacion producing steam producing water (reaction (1)+(2) below)
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{  r r l r }
(1)&\ce{CO2_{(g)} + 4H2_{(g)} & <=> \: CH4_{(g)} + 2\Cancel{H2O_{(g)}}  }&$K_{c}(A\ce{<=>}B)=\frac{   \big[ \ce{CH4} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{H2O} \big]^2    } {  \big[ \ce{CO2} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{H2} \big]^4    }$ \\
\\
(2)&\ce{2\Cancel{H2O_{(g)}}  & -> \: 2H2O_{(l)} }&$K_{c}(B\ce{<=>}C)= \frac{1}{ \big[ \ce{H2O} \big]^2 }  $ \\
\multicolumn{4}{r}{} \rule{13cm}{0.4pt}\\
(1)+(2)&  \ce{ CO2_{(g)} + 4H2_{(g)} &-> \: CH4_{(g)} + 2H2O_{(l)} } &$K_{c}(A\ce{<=>}C)=\frac{   \big[ \ce{CH4} \big]      } {  \big[ \ce{CO2} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{H2} \big]^4    }$\\
 \end{tabular}
 \end{center}
By comparing the reactions above we can see that
\begin{equation*}
 \boxed{ 
 K_{c}(A\ce{<=>}C)=K_{c}(A\ce{<=>}B)\cdot K_{c}(B\ce{<=>}C)
 }
\end{equation*}
Therefore, adding two equilibrium would multiply its corresponding equilibrium constants.
